# Memory Bank

Context does not persist across sessions. Treat the **Memory Bank** as the durable project brain: at the **start of every non-trivial task**, read **all** Markdown files under `memory-bank/` (use the read tool) **before** planning or coding. Skipping this is not optional when those files exist.

## New chats and file contents

This rule is **always applied** (instructions are in context), but **the bodies of `memory-bank/*.md` are not auto-loaded** into the model. **Actually read** each core file at task start, or ask the user to **`@memory-bank`** / `@memory-bank/activeContext.md` (and siblings) so content is attached. Do not assume prior turns’ file contents are still present.

## Location

All Memory Bank files live in **`memory-bank/`** at the repository root (Markdown). Create the folder and core files during project initialization if they are missing.

## Structure (hierarchy)

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    PB[projectbrief.md] --> PC[productContext.md]
    PB --> SP[systemPatterns.md]
    PB --> TC[techContext.md]
    PC --> AC[activeContext.md]
    SP --> AC
    TC --> AC
    AC --> P[progress.md]
```

### Core files (required)

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `projectbrief.md` | Foundation: scope, goals, requirements; source of truth for what “in scope” means |
| `productContext.md` | Why the product exists, problems solved, expected behavior, UX goals |
| `activeContext.md` | Current focus, recent changes, next steps, active decisions |
| `systemPatterns.md` | Architecture, key technical decisions, patterns, component relationships |
| `techContext.md` | Stack, dev setup, constraints, major dependencies |
| `progress.md` | What works, what is left, status, known issues |

### Additional context (optional)

Add files or subfolders under `memory-bank/` when useful: feature specs, integrations, API notes, testing or deployment docs.

## Workflows

### Plan-first flow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Start[Start] --> ReadFiles[Read Memory Bank]
    ReadFiles --> CheckFiles{Files complete enough?}
    CheckFiles -->|No| Plan[Create / gap-fill plan]
    Plan --> Document[Document in chat]
    CheckFiles -->|Yes| Verify[Verify context]
    Verify --> Strategy[Develop strategy]
    Strategy --> Present[Present approach]
```

### Execution flow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Start[Start] --> Context[Read / refresh Memory Bank]
    Context --> Update[Update Memory Bank as needed]
    Update --> Rules[Update .cursor/rules if durable patterns warrant it]
    Rules --> Execute[Execute task]
    Execute --> Document[Document changes in Memory Bank]
```

Prefer updating **existing** project rules (e.g. `backend-python.mdc`, `frontend-web.mdc`) rather than inventing duplicate sources of truth. Propose rule edits explicitly when they change team-wide behavior.

## When to update the Memory Bank

1. Discovering new project-wide patterns or decisions  
2. After significant implementation milestones  
3. When the user says **update memory bank** — then **review every** Memory Bank file (even if some need no edits); prioritize `activeContext.md` and `progress.md`  
4. When context is unclear and docs would prevent repeated rediscovery  

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Start[Update process]
    subgraph Process
        P1[Review ALL files]
        P2[Document current state]
        P3[Clarify next steps]
        P4[Adjust .cursor/rules only if warranted]
        P1 --> P2 --> P3 --> P4
    end
    Start --> Process
```

## Project intelligence (this repo)

Long-lived preferences and “how we work here” belong in **`.cursor/rules/*.mdc`** (and `README` / PRDs where applicable). If a root **`.cursorrules`** file exists in a workspace, treat it as optional extra project memory—do not duplicate large sections of Memory Bank inside rules unless the user asks.

## Planner mode

When the user asks for **Planner Mode** or uses **`/plan`**:

1. Reflect on the request and **inspect relevant code** to bound scope.  
2. Before proposing a plan, ask **4–6 clarifying questions** grounded in what you found.  
3. After answers, produce a **comprehensive plan** and wait for **explicit approval**.  
4. After approval, implement the plan; after each phase or major step, state **what was completed**, **what comes next**, and **remaining phases**.

This complements **PRD-gated workflows** if present: approval and explicit execution still apply per those rules.

